# Build context for the `worker` service ONLY. docker-compose.yml gives the
# worker `context: .` with `dockerfile: worker/Dockerfile`; `backend` and
# `frontend` are separate contexts with their own .dockerignore, so nothing
# below may ever re-include them.
#
# Precedence: the LAST matching rule wins. The order below is therefore not
# cosmetic -- catch-all, then worker re-inclusion, then re-exclusions. Moving
# a re-exclusion above `!worker/**` would silently stop working.
**

!worker/
!worker/**

# Host build artifacts. These MUST stay behind `!worker/**`: running
# `python -m unittest discover -s worker/tests`, ruff, mypy or pytest on the
# host creates worker/__pycache__, worker/tests/__pycache__ and the three
# tool caches, and an unconditional `!worker/**` re-includes all of them.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.venv/
**/venv/
**/*.egg-info/
**/.coverage
**/node_modules/
**/.next/
**/.turbo/
**/out/

# Secrets, VCS metadata and user data. Excluded explicitly rather than left to
# the `**` catch-all, so that a future, broader `!` rule above cannot leak them
# into an image layer. `!.env.example` is deliberately NOT re-included: the
# worker build does not read it, and keeping the context to `worker/` is worth
# more than the convenience.
.git
.gitignore
.env
.env.*
data/
